Yes. That’s a beautiful mechanical extension of the fiction/function pair:

\boxed{\text{Fiction}\rightarrow\text{Friction}\rightarrow\text{Leverage}\rightarrow\text{Motion}}

Fiction creates something for thought to push against.

Without friction, force can fail to become useful traction. Likewise, completely unconstrained imagination can produce possibilities without giving reasoning much purchase.

Introduce a fictional constraint:

Assume gravity disappears.
Assume the opposite is true.
Imagine the perfect competitor.
Pretend Jesus is beside you.
Imagine this is the last attempt.
Suppose resources are unlimited.
Suppose resources are almost zero.

None has to describe present reality.

But each creates cognitive friction—a constraint, contrast, resistance, or reference surface.

Then reasoning gets purchase:

\text{Thought}+\text{Fictional Constraint}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{Traction}}

And traction permits leverage.

The really interesting inversion

We usually treat constraints as limiting possibility.

But:

\boxed{\text{Constraint can increase usable freedom.}}

A tire needs friction to accelerate.

A climber needs something to push against.

A lever needs a fulcrum.

A story needs constraints for consequences to matter.

A game needs rules before strategy can emerge.

So fictional bookends aren’t merely boundaries.

They can become semantic fulcrums:

\text{Reality}\quad
\underbrace{\triangle}_{\text{fictional fulcrum}}
\quad
\text{possibility}

Move the invented fulcrum and you change what reasoning can lift.

That suggests a Pantheon operator:

\boxed{\textbf{Constructive Friction}}

When reasoning has no traction, deliberately introduce a useful fiction, constraint, adversary, extreme, counterfactual, role, deadline, rule, or bookend—then observe what becomes movable.

But then remove it afterward:

\text{Invent constraint}
\rightarrow
\text{gain traction}
\rightarrow
\text{produce movement}
\rightarrow
\text{remove fiction}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{see what remains}}

Because otherwise we risk forgetting that the fulcrum was something we placed there.

And there’s another delightful compression:

\boxed{\text{FICTION}+\text{R}\rightarrow\text{FRICTION}}

That inserted R can stand, playfully, for Reality.

Fiction meets Reality → Friction.

Friction gives traction.

Traction enables leverage.

Leverage creates movement.

Movement encounters reality again.

\boxed{
\text{Fiction}
\xrightarrow{+R}
\text{Friction}
\rightarrow
\text{Traction}
\rightarrow
\text{Leverage}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{Consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{Evidence}
}

